𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟵/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗶𝗻 𝗖𝗜/𝗖𝗗 & 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗹-𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗹𝗱 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀

Continuing my #30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on one of the most practical use cases of Ansible — integrating it with CI/CD pipelines.

In real-world DevOps environments, Ansible is rarely used manually. It is often triggered automatically by CI/CD tools after code is tested and approved.

🔄 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗙𝗶𝘁𝘀 𝗶𝗻𝘁𝗼 𝗖𝗜/𝗖𝗗

A typical flow looks like:

Developer
   ↓
Git Push
   ↓
CI Pipeline
   ↓
Build & Test
   ↓
Ansible Playbook
   ↓
Deploy
   ↓
Verify

⚙️ 𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲

A CI pipeline can execute:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini deploy.yml

The playbook could then deploy an application:

- name: Deploy Application
  hosts: app_servers
  become: true

  tasks:
    - name: Pull latest application code
      ansible.builtin.git:
        repo: https://lnkd.in/gZRaTuDd
        dest: /opt/app
        version: main

    - name: Restart application
      ansible.builtin.service:
        name: myapp
        state: restarted

🚀 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗹-𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗹𝗱 𝗨𝘀𝗲 𝗖𝗮𝘀𝗲𝘀

Ansible can be used in CI/CD for:

✅ Application deployments
✅ Server configuration
✅ Rolling deployments
✅ Environment provisioning
✅ Configuration updates
✅ Post-deployment validation
✅ Automated rollback workflows

🔐 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗠𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘀

Never hardcode passwords, API keys, or other secrets inside playbooks.

Use Ansible Vault or integrate Ansible with your organization's secret-management solution.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Ansible becomes much more powerful when it moves from manual execution → automated CI/CD workflows.

The goal isn't just to automate deployment.

It's to make deployments repeatable, consistent, and reliable. 🚀
